all selected rows, correct? We did add testtag to comer. Not tested adding to multiple rows; would add tag \"testtag\" to both comer and mesa, then we'd remove. But the dialog applied perhaps only to the \"selected\" row? Let me test this behavior: select comer+mesa, open tags dialog with verb checked (union), uncheck verb, confirm → removes verb from comer (and nothing from mesa). Then re-add via dialog. That tests applying tags to all selected rows. But we already did an analogous operation: added testtag to comer (single). Multiple-row application might have a bug in the changed build (only apply to first row?). Is that in scope? The changed feature is about checkbox state from union, not applying. But the tags area generally... A bug where applying tags to multiple rows only affects one would be significant. Let me test it, then restore.\n\nTest plan: select comer+mesa → Edit tags → currently testtag absent (deleted), verb checked. Uncheck verb → Confirm. Then check: comer should no longer have verb; mesa had none. Then to be thorough, verify tag list still shows verb? (unused tag persists). Then re-add verb to both? Wait, we want restore precondition: comer tagged verb, mesa untagged. So after confirming removal (verb off both), select comer only → open tags dialog → check verb → Confirm → comer tagged verb again. Then cleanup unused tags? The verb tag won't disappear because re-used. Fine.\n\nBut does the confirm apply to both selected? Since neither had verb after, both unchanged. Hmm that test doesn't prove multi-row application. Better: select comer+mesa, open dialog, check verb (already checked), then Confirm with no change → it would add verb to mesa (since checked) and keep comer → both tagged. Then verify mesa now has verb (select mesa only → dialog shows verb checked). Then remove verb from mesa: select mesa only → open dialog, uncheck verb, Confirm → mesa untagged. Then verify. This tests multi-row apply of \"add tag to all selected\". Let me do that;