{"type": "thinking", "text": "Selection exited. Now test stale query when browsing a deck:\n1. Search \"hola\" submit → 1 card.\n2. Exit browser (BACK) while search bar open? The browser state should save. Actually pressing BACK while search bar is open might first collapse the search bar (like closing search), not exit. Hmm. Earlier pressing BACK from the browser with tags dialog open returned to selection etc. Let me search then collapse search? If I collapse search, query clears (as seen, collapse shows all). Hmm, that would clear the query before saving.\n\nBetter: search \"hola\", then press BACK. First BACK likely closes the search bar (collapse), clearing it; second BACK exits browser. Then no stale query. Hmm, so testing stale query requires leaving the browser via the up arrow (Navigate up) while the search bar is open? The up arrow might exit the activity with the query saved. Let's try: search hola, then tap \"Navigate up\" (element 0 in toolbar is the up arrow when search open? In observe with search open, element 0 was ImageButton desc=\"Collapse\" @(73,146) — the up arrow becomes collapse). Hmm, so can't exit via up while search bar open.\n\nAlternative route: use the drawer to navigate away (open drawer → Decks) while search is open. That might save browser state with query. Hmm, but the browser activity pauses and saves state with the current query \"hola\". Then deck list → long-press French → Browse cards. The browser may resume the same activity instead of recreating? Long-press Browse cards starts CardBrowserActivity with a deck; if an existing instance is in the back stack, it might bypass.\n\nThis is getting speculative. The changed feature is specifically the deck dropdown starting deck. Let me test the stale-query scenario in a simple way:\n- Search \"hola\" in browser (All decks) → 1 card.\n- Open drawer (element 0? no, with search open, element 0 is Collapse; the drawer hamburger is replaced). Hmm, with search open, the toolbar shows Collapse (back arrow) instead of hamburger. So to leave the browser, I could press HOME and reopen? The activity state persists but not exited. Hmm.\n- Alternatively, press BACK twice: first collapses search (query cleared/ignored), second exits. So query state saved as cleared. Stale query unlikely.\n\nActually, does the card browser save the search query in its state? The changed feature mentions \"browser state\" — AnkiDroid's browser state includes deck, search terms, sort order maybe. If the user typed a query and then collapsed the search bar (which triggers a new empty search?), then the saved query is empty. So no stale query.\n\nHmm, what about navigating via drawer while search is open? The drawer isn't accessible then.\n\nI'll skip the stale query test; it's likely not the planted bug.\n\nLet me instead check the browser state behavior more precisely for the deck dropdown bug: Does the browser remember the deck filter across app restart? Earlier we saw Spanish persisted across navigation and app exit. That's expected (browser state). Good.\n\nNow let me think about \"Deck list toolbar: deck search results are now computed as each character is typed.\" Perhaps this refers to the deck picker *within the add-note screen* (when choosing a deck for a new note) — the deck list toolbar there has a filter icon too. Hmm, \"deck list toolbar\" could be the Filter decks search in the deck picker dialogs across the app (they share the same component). We tested one instance in the browser. Maybe the bug appears in another instance (e.g., when adding a note and choosing deck)? Possible but unlikely; same component.\n\nLet me test the deck picker filter from the \"Add note\" deck selection (or from deck overview's \"Select deck\"). Actually, the deck picker dialog with filter is the same component. Fine.\n\nWhat about the main deck list screen: in newer AnkiDroid, there is a search icon in the deck list app bar (magnifier) that opens a search field filtering the deck list. Our build's deck list toolbar lacks it—maybe it's hidden because the \"deck list toolbar\" means the deck picker dialog. OK.\n\nLet me now do the rotation test on the card browser (quick) and then consider remaining behaviors: e.g., the card browser deck filter after rotation; the search bar state after rotation; the tag dialog we already rotated.\n\nLet me rotate the browser to landscape and verify 7 cards and deck filter All decks."}
