{"type": "thinking", "text": "Back at deck list. No data changed (empty note discarded). Everything restored.\n\nNow, final consideration: Should I report the \"Default\" initial browser deck state? Let me think about it more carefully because it might be a real issue tied to the changed feature:\n\nAt app start, Card Browser from drawer → \"Default\" | \"0 cards shown\". The deck picker listed no Default. If the user opens the card browser fresh, showing an empty \"Default\" deck is arguably the app's default behavior in this build. But is it a regression? The change \"its starting deck is now loaded from browser state ahead of the launching screen's request\" could produce this if browser state defaults to deckId 1. But a default of deck 1 is probably pre-existing.\n\nHmm, but hold on: maybe the initial state wasn't default—maybe the test harness pre-set browser state to Default deliberately? The preconditions only describe decks and tags. A \"Default\" deck with 0 cards is implied (Anki always has a Default deck if collection created normally, but it may not be shown because... hmm, it should be shown in deck list if it exists). Wait, if Default deck exists, the deck list should show \"Default 0 0 0\" unless empty decks are hidden. We created an empty \"Sub\" deck and it appeared. So empty decks are NOT hidden. Therefore, if Default existed, it would appear in the deck list. It doesn't. So the \"Default\" deck does NOT exist in the collection (or was deleted). Yet the browser filter \"Default\" with 0 cards refers to a non-existent deck id 1.\n\nIn Anki's collection, deck id 1 (Default) always exists unless deleted. The preconditions say decks Spanish and French; maybe the collection's Default deck was deleted during setup. If deleted, the browser state's saved deckId=1 is stale. The changed feature loads browser state ahead of request, so it shows \"Default\" (fallback name for id 1) with 0 cards. That's arguably a bug: opening the card browser shows a deck that no longer exists, hiding all cards. Expected: fall back to All decks (or the requested deck).\n\nBut wait, was that observed on the *first* browser open in this session? Yes. Browser state at that point was whatever persisted in shared prefs. The test environment may have had a saved browser state with deckId=1 from a prior session (e.g., before decks were created). The change \"starting deck loaded from browser state\" would then show the stale Default deck. Hmm, that sounds like a legitimate bug: stale browser state deck is not validated. But the changed feature's bug we found already covers state overriding the request. This \"Default\" case is state overriding the \"no request\" default. Is the expected behavior \"All decks\"? Unclear.\n\nActually, wait. Let's reconsider: the first browser open showed \"Default\" | \"0 cards shown\". Then I tapped the deck filter; the dialog listed \"All decks\", \"French\", \"Spanish\" — no \"Default\". So the filter name \"Default\" is not selectable. This is inconsistent UI: the toolbar shows a deck filter (\"Default\") that is not in the picker. Could be reported as a bug: the deck dropdown starts with a deck that doesn't exist / isn't listed, showing 0 cards. But is it caused by the changed feature? The change says starting deck loaded from browser state. Hmm.\n\nHowever, notice: after I set the filter to \"All decks\" and used the browser, the state updated. On a fresh install with a normal collection, the browser state initial deck might be Default deck which exists (id 1) with 0 cards—so stock behavior would also show Default/0 cards on first open. In AnkiDroid, is that plausible? Actually, when you open the card browser for the first time, I believe it shows all cards (\"All decks\"). Hmm.\n\nI need to decide whether to report. The developer confirmed one bug; filing an uncertain one risks rejection but the instruction says confirmed reports are logged; rejected ones presumably not counted. The task says a report about something missing may leave the quote out; a quote never on screen gets rejected. This quote (\"Default\" | \"0 cards shown\") was on screen at session start (element 1: LinearLayout contains \"Default\" | \"0 cards shown\"). So technically valid.\n\nBut is it a bug? The expected behavior is debatable. Since the deck picker can't select Default, and the deck list doesn't show it, showing \"Default\" as the browser filter with 0 cards is inconsistent. But the user can easily pick All decks. It might be stock behavior (browser default deck id = 1).\n\nLet me test what happens now: If I clear app data... can't. The browser state now is All decks, so reopening won't show Default. The initial state was likely persisted prefs from previous test runs (maybe the harness left browser state deckId=1). This isn't necessarily this build's bug.\n\nThe changed feature's known bug is what we reported. The rest appears fine. Let me not report ambiguous stuff.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider the deck dropdown bug's expected behavior more precisely for the report: Is it possible that the intended behavior of the change is exactly \"browser state takes precedence over the launching request\" (as the changelog says \"loaded from browser state ahead of the launching screen's request\")? If so, the developer would have rejected. But the developer confirmed it, so it's a bug. Good.\n\nHmm, the wording \"loaded from browser state ahead of the launching screen's request\" could mean \"load browser state first, then the request is applied after\" (ahead in execution order, not priority). The bug is that the request isn't applied. Whatever—confirmed.\n\nNow, one more check: maybe there's a bug with the deck *search* when typing quickly (debounce). We typed via type_text which types a whole string instantly and each call updated. Fine.\n\nLet me also consider the tag dialog union with \"Check/uncheck all tags\" combined with filter: When filtering tags to \"zzz\", then \"Check/uncheck all tags\" — what happens? It might toggle all tags including hidden ones, or none. Not in changed scope. Skip.\n\nI believe we've done a thorough sweep. Let me do a final data integrity check and sign off.\n\nFinal check: deck list shows French 3, Spanish 4; tag \"verb\" on comer only; no extra tags; no extra decks; no extra cards. Let me verify card count via browser All decks = 7. And deck list. Already confirmed. Let me also check that the cards' contents are unchanged (comer/to eat? we didn't modify fields). We never saved any note edits (only tag changes, which we reverted). Good.\n\nOne more: The tag \"verb\" tag's checkbox state for comer was verified after cleanup? We verified mesa untagged. Let me verify comer still tagged after check database: we saw earlier at 8:44 that mesa had verb after confirm, then removed; didn't re-verify comer after the check database. Let me quickly check: browser long-press comer → Edit tags → verb checked. Also verify no noun. Let me do this quickly.\n\nGo to browser from drawer."}
